{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact O1 head 4b86017a479d52f5aef37b8014c5942c6eb7f099 on builder/oauth-resource-20261102, tree a1060ed6, for request a8f28a37 under promise 0a268510 and review request e5970602. This approval covers the resource server: discovery, the challenge, access-token admission and grant-owned transports. It claims no issuer, consent, registration, code exchange, refresh, revocation endpoint, deployment or live host.\n\nScope. Main 5f67f6ae is an ancestor, and the worktree is clean. The head changes 23 paths, and the commit-message check passes for all eight commits. No card path changes: hosted.ts, http.ts and worker.ts sit outside the card list, and service.ts and views/** are untouched. git diff 1ca60fee HEAD lists only the note, the two manifests and the landed sprint report, so production source, tests and configuration equal the gated checkpoint. Two live docs/mcp.md artifacts sit off main, f12a28a7 and 52c69ba1, from the 13 October Counter promise 3bb70eab. These are the siblings that the hosted-cards landing left under 0ad4e770.\n\nResource identity. configuredMcpResources accepts at most eight distinct canonical HTTPS /mcp URLs, each at most 2,048 bytes, in a setting of at most 32,800 bytes, with no credentials or fragment. hostedMcpResources requires every MCP_CARD_ORIGINS URL to appear in that list. An invalid setting makes hostedMcpTools return null, so mcpRoute answers 404 before any body read or namespace lookup. The metadata route answers only for a configured URL, with the query kept. That URL becomes its resource, and an unmatched query answers 404. publicRequestUrl joins the operator's origin to the request's path and query. readPublicOrigin falls back only to the request's own origin, so a forwarding header cannot choose the audience. A legacy HTTP service advertises no resource, and mcpChallengeResponse adds no header for a non-HTTPS URL.\n\nAdmission. A token reads agent.grant.secret. inspect checks, in order:\n\n- the 43-character grant ID and secret;\n- an enrolled agent;\n- an active row of at most 4,096 bytes, with exactly seven fields and bounded times;\n- the expiry;\n- grant.resource equal to the exact public request URL;\n- the digest, compared over its full length.\n\nOnly then does standing read the Native authority. It requires an active key and member, the agent's own handle, key.controller equal to the grant's controller, and an active controller. The cache measures 60 seconds from the start of the read. After every await, current() rereads the durable row, the agent's identity and the clock. An inactive answer ends the grant, and an unavailable answer is not cached. Controls 35 and 36 each fail their intended assertion when the audience or controller check is removed.\n\nTransports and limits. Each grant opens its own McpHttp, restores only its own oauth_session rows, and closes when its row changes or its deadline passes. While a standing refresh awaits, a separate timer still closes the layer at the earlier deadline, so a late answer cannot extend a stream. fetch rechecks admission after the layer opens and requires the same grant row before dispatch. Static and OAuth streams share the agent's four stream slots, and the two session tables share its sixteen sessions. When all sixteen sessions are held, a new session for a credential that holds fewer than sixteen fails with SessionFull and evicts nothing of another credential's. Bearer rotation keeps OAuth layers. Retirement ends every grant, deletes every OAuth session, refunds their charges and bumps the OAuth epoch.\n\nGate and evidence. gate.json records head 1ca60fee and tree 55bb1505, before and after, with a clean status, and gate.exit reads exit 0. The focused manifest hashes to 2e5f5191…, and all 75 files match their sizes and digests and are read-only. The gate manifest hashes to ca2ebaf3…, and all 9 files match. The 72 and 31 private references in their run records all match their files. Neither unit holds an unlisted file, and the scan for private keys and GitHub tokens finds no match. The six failed focused runs stay on the record with their corrections. The cancellation observation in runs 25 and 26 stays qualified as the note says.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Admission stays in the agent object beside the static bearer, and authority stays Native. Each grant owns its transport, and the deployment policy decides the audience.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. No step widens authority. A wrong connector, digest, expiry, row or controller answers 401 before or without dispatch. The deployed Workers remain closed.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Two items to settle before O4 connects a live host:\n\n1. Unavailable authority answers like a bad token. When the Native read fails, #authorized returns unauthorized, so the host receives 401 with error=\"invalid_token\". The failure also counts in the per-address window that static bearers share. RFC 6750 uses invalid_token for an expired, revoked or malformed token. So a membership outage tells Claude or ChatGPT to discard a token that is still valid, and repeated calls move that address toward 429. A 503 with Retry-After and no failure charge would keep the grant usable through an outage. Decide this in O3, where refresh and the host's reaction meet.\n2. A ninth grant's first call closes the oldest grant's layer and its streams. All grants on one agent share one controller, so the eviction stays within one person's connectors, and the sessions stay durable. Set O3's live-grant limit at or below OAUTH_GRANTS_MOST, so that live grants never evict each other.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:55d496e123c72b09dc25133fd497acc0868c09e2","binding":"assigned","head":"4b86017a479d52f5aef37b8014c5942c6eb7f099","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a83fe38b508f44bc333bc590ce353d060fd02cb6\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736